From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Waltteri Hooli <wahooli@localhost>
Date: Fri, 12 Sep 2026 19:00:00 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 85/85] local: pinctrl: qcom: restore the gpio pad registers
 after hibernation

Bluetooth does not work after a cross-boot hibernate resume. The controller is
the WCN7850's UART half on uart14, hci_qca over serdev, enabled by the
wcn7850-pmu power sequencer through TLMM gpio116.

Measured with /sys/kernel/debug/gpio, which is a real hardware read --
msm_gpio_dbg_show_one() does MMIO reads of the ctl and io registers rather than
reporting a cached value. On a boot that never hibernated, with Bluetooth first
off and then on:

  gpio116 : out low  func0 2mA no pull
  gpio116 : out high func0 2mA no pull

After a cross-boot resume, with Bluetooth off exactly as it was at freeze:

  gpio116 : in  low  func0 2mA pull down

Input with a pull-down: the TLMM power-on default. The output enable is gone.
pwrseq_qcom_wcn then asserts the line with gpiod_set_value_cansleep(bt_gpio, 1),
which reaches msm_gpio_set() -- and that writes the io register and nothing
else. The pad never drives, the Bluetooth half of the chip stays in reset, and
qca_setup() fails the way a chip that is not there fails:

  Bluetooth: hci0: setting up wcn7850
  Bluetooth: hci0: command 0xfc00 tx timeout
  Bluetooth: hci0: Reading QCA version information failed (-110)
  Bluetooth: hci0: Retry BT power ON:0

Nothing replays ctl or io. pinctrl_bind_pins() runs once, at probe.
msm_pinctrl_resume() calls pinctrl_force_default(), which selects
pctldev->hog_default -- hogs only, not consumer states. And gpiolib keeps no
record of a line's output value, so even a direction replay could not put the
level back. Same defect class as the companion patch 0079, one register over:
0079 restores intr_cfg, this restores ctl and io.

Why only this pin. Pins with a second owner come back correct and hid the bug
for a while. uart14's own pins are right after a resume -- gpio56-59, func1,
carrying the exact bias of qup_uart14_default down to the keeper on CTS and the
pull-up on RX -- because the boot kernel probes a98000.serial before it loads
the image, and pinctrl_bind_pins() muxes them then. gpio117, WLAN enable, reads
"out high 16mA": driven, but at a drive strength no DT here asks for
(wcn_wlan_bt_en says 2mA), so the boot firmware asserted it, which is why Wi-Fi
survives a resume and Bluetooth does not. gpio116 has neither owner and is left
at reset values.

Save ctl and io for every valid gpio at .freeze/.poweroff, write them back at
.restore_noirq. Every value written is one this kernel itself programmed, so a
pin some other agent already got right is written back identically, and a pin
nobody owns is corrected. io is written before ctl so a pin regaining its output
enable already holds the right level and does not glitch the pad.

The save runs before pinctrl_force_sleep(), so the image carries the active
configuration rather than the hogs' sleep state; msm_pinctrl_resume() runs
pinctrl_force_default() on the way out and puts the hogs back.

Pads before irq config in .restore_noirq: an interrupt is configured on a line
whose mux and direction have to be right first, and 0079's replay writes
intr_cfg only. Still .restore_noirq, for 0079's reason -- ahead of
resume_device_irqs().

SET_SYSTEM_SLEEP_PM_OPS() is expanded by hand because .freeze and .poweroff now
differ from .suspend. Suspend/resume keep the plain callbacks: only the
hibernate path has anything to put back.

Not board-specific: msm_pinctrl_dev_pm_ops is shared by every qcom pinctrl
driver, so this covers all of them. On x1e80100 it costs 2 * 4 * 300 bytes in
struct msm_pinctrl and no allocation on the freeze path.

Signed-off-by: Waltteri Hooli <wahooli@localhost>
---
 drivers/pinctrl/qcom/pinctrl-msm.c | 138 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 137 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/drivers/pinctrl/qcom/pinctrl-msm.c
+++ b/drivers/pinctrl/qcom/pinctrl-msm.c
@@ -58,6 +58,10 @@
  * @soc:            Reference to soc_data of platform specific data.
  * @regs:           Base addresses for the TLMM tiles.
  * @phys_base:      Physical base address
+ * @pm_ctl:         Hibernate: saved ctl register (mux, drive, bias, output
+ *                  enable) per gpio.
+ * @pm_io:          Hibernate: saved io register (output level) per gpio.
+ * @pm_saved:       Hibernate: pm_ctl/pm_io hold a snapshot taken at freeze.
  */
 struct msm_pinctrl {
 	struct device *dev;
@@ -80,6 +84,10 @@
 	const struct msm_pinctrl_soc_data *soc;
 	void __iomem *regs[MAX_NR_TILES];
 	u32 phys_base[MAX_NR_TILES];
+
+	u32 pm_ctl[MAX_NR_GPIO];
+	u32 pm_io[MAX_NR_GPIO];
+	bool pm_saved;
 };
 
 #define MSM_ACCESSOR(name) \
@@ -1535,6 +1543,86 @@
 }
 
 /*
+ * Hibernate: the pad configuration lives in two registers that nothing replays
+ * after a cross-boot restore.
+ *
+ * ctl holds the mux, drive strength, bias AND the output enable; io holds the
+ * output level. Both come back from the reboot at power-on values while every
+ * driver that programmed them comes back from the image believing otherwise,
+ * and nothing rewrites them. pinctrl_bind_pins() runs once, at probe.
+ * msm_pinctrl_resume()'s pinctrl_force_default() re-applies only HOGS -- it
+ * selects pctldev->hog_default, not consumer states. And gpiolib caches no
+ * output value at all, so there is nothing to replay from: msm_gpio_set()
+ * writes the io register and never touches ctl.
+ *
+ * Measured on x1e80100. The WCN7850's Bluetooth enable is TLMM gpio116, driven
+ * by the wcn7850-pmu power sequencer. On a boot that never hibernated, with
+ * Bluetooth first off and then on:
+ *
+ *   gpio116 : out low  func0 2mA no pull
+ *   gpio116 : out high func0 2mA no pull
+ *
+ * After a cross-boot resume, with Bluetooth off exactly as it was at freeze:
+ *
+ *   gpio116 : in  low  func0 2mA pull down
+ *
+ * Input, with a pull-down: the power-on default. The output enable is gone, so
+ * pwrseq_qcom_wcn's gpiod_set_value_cansleep(bt_gpio, 1) writes an io register
+ * that reaches no pad. The Bluetooth half of the chip stays in reset and
+ * qca_setup() fails the way a chip that is not there fails:
+ *
+ *   Bluetooth: hci0: setting up wcn7850
+ *   Bluetooth: hci0: command 0xfc00 tx timeout
+ *   Bluetooth: hci0: Reading QCA version information failed (-110)
+ *
+ * Only pins with no second owner are affected, which is what made this hard to
+ * see. uart14's own pins come back correct -- gpio56-59, func1, carrying the
+ * exact bias of qup_uart14_default down to the keeper on CTS and the pull-up on
+ * RX -- because the boot kernel probes a98000.serial before it loads the image
+ * and pinctrl_bind_pins() muxes them then. gpio117, WLAN enable, comes back
+ * "out high 16mA": driven, but at a drive strength this DT never asks for
+ * (wcn_wlan_bt_en says 2mA), so the boot firmware asserted it. gpio116 has
+ * neither, and is the one pin left at reset values.
+ *
+ * So save both registers at freeze and write them back at restore. Every value
+ * written is one this kernel itself programmed, so a pin some other agent got
+ * right is written back identically and a pin nobody owns is corrected.
+ */
+static void msm_pinctrl_save_pads(struct msm_pinctrl *pctrl)
+{
+	unsigned long flags;
+	unsigned int i;
+
+	for (i = 0; i < pctrl->chip.ngpio; i++) {
+		const struct msm_pingroup *g = &pctrl->soc->groups[i];
+
+		if (!gpiochip_line_is_valid(&pctrl->chip, i))
+			continue;
+
+		raw_spin_lock_irqsave(&pctrl->lock, flags);
+		pctrl->pm_ctl[i] = msm_readl_ctl(pctrl, g);
+		pctrl->pm_io[i] = msm_readl_io(pctrl, g);
+		raw_spin_unlock_irqrestore(&pctrl->lock, flags);
+	}
+
+	pctrl->pm_saved = true;
+}
+
+static int __maybe_unused msm_pinctrl_freeze(struct device *dev)
+{
+	struct msm_pinctrl *pctrl = dev_get_drvdata(dev);
+
+	/*
+	 * Before pinctrl_force_sleep(), so the image carries the active
+	 * configuration rather than the hogs' sleep state. msm_pinctrl_resume()
+	 * runs pinctrl_force_default() on the way out and puts those back.
+	 */
+	msm_pinctrl_save_pads(pctrl);
+
+	return msm_pinctrl_suspend(dev);
+}
+
+/*
  * Hibernate restore: TLMM comes back from the reboot with its per-interrupt
  * configuration at power-on values, while irq_desc comes back from the image
  * still describing what was programmed at request time.
@@ -1581,6 +1669,43 @@
 	unsigned int i;
 
 	/*
+	 * The pads first: an interrupt is configured on a line whose mux and
+	 * direction have to be right, and 0079's irq_set_irq_type() replay
+	 * below writes intr_cfg only.
+	 */
+	for (i = 0; pctrl->pm_saved && i < pctrl->chip.ngpio; i++) {
+		const struct msm_pingroup *g = &pctrl->soc->groups[i];
+		u32 ctl, io;
+		unsigned long flags;
+		bool changed;
+
+		if (!gpiochip_line_is_valid(&pctrl->chip, i))
+			continue;
+
+		raw_spin_lock_irqsave(&pctrl->lock, flags);
+		ctl = msm_readl_ctl(pctrl, g);
+		io = msm_readl_io(pctrl, g);
+		/*
+		 * in_bit is the live pad level and tracks the outside world, so
+		 * only out_bit can be meaningfully compared or restored.
+		 */
+		changed = ctl != pctrl->pm_ctl[i] ||
+			  ((io ^ pctrl->pm_io[i]) & BIT(g->out_bit));
+		if (changed) {
+			/*
+			 * io before ctl: a pin regaining its output enable
+			 * already holds the right level, so the pad does not
+			 * glitch on the way through.
+			 */
+			io &= ~BIT(g->out_bit);
+			io |= pctrl->pm_io[i] & BIT(g->out_bit);
+			msm_writel_io(io, pctrl, g);
+			msm_writel_ctl(pctrl->pm_ctl[i], pctrl, g);
+		}
+		raw_spin_unlock_irqrestore(&pctrl->lock, flags);
+	}
+
+	/*
 	 * Every gpio with a mapped irq, NOT just pctrl->enabled_irqs.
 	 * msm_gpio_irq_mask() clears that bitmap and suspend_device_irqs()
 	 * has masked every non-wakeup interrupt before we get here, so by
@@ -1628,8 +1753,19 @@
 	return 0;
 }
 
+/*
+ * SET_SYSTEM_SLEEP_PM_OPS() expanded by hand: the hibernate entries have to
+ * differ from the suspend ones. .freeze and .poweroff snapshot the pads, and
+ * only the hibernate path has anything to put back, so suspend/resume keep the
+ * plain callbacks and stay exactly as they were.
+ */
 const struct dev_pm_ops msm_pinctrl_dev_pm_ops = {
-	SET_SYSTEM_SLEEP_PM_OPS(msm_pinctrl_suspend, msm_pinctrl_resume)
+	.suspend = pm_sleep_ptr(msm_pinctrl_suspend),
+	.resume = pm_sleep_ptr(msm_pinctrl_resume),
+	.freeze = pm_sleep_ptr(msm_pinctrl_freeze),
+	.thaw = pm_sleep_ptr(msm_pinctrl_resume),
+	.poweroff = pm_sleep_ptr(msm_pinctrl_freeze),
+	.restore = pm_sleep_ptr(msm_pinctrl_resume),
 	.restore_noirq = pm_sleep_ptr(msm_pinctrl_restore_noirq),
 };
 
